========================================================================
SECTION 3: BUILDING PROCESS
========================================================================

3.1 ANALYSE THE RAW LOG STRUCTURE

Examine the sample log and identify:
  - Format: JSON, syslog-wrapped positional, CSV, key-value pairs, or other
  - Top-level columns: fields already parsed by the XSIAM ingest pipeline
  - Nested fields: data inside JSON strings or raw_log
  - _raw_log availability: some datasets have an empty _raw_log column. The parser extracts fields into top-level columns instead
  - Data types: strings vs numbers vs timestamps vs IPs
  - Event type discrimination: does a single field split logs into different subtypes

3.2 CHOOSE THE EXTRACTION STRATEGY

Decision tree:

  If _raw_log contains valid JSON:
    Use json_extract_scalar(_raw_log, "$.path")

  If _raw_log is empty but top-level columns are JSON strings:
    Use json_extract_scalar(to_string(column_name), "$.path")

  If _raw_log is empty and fields are direct top-level columns:
    Use column names directly (e.g. senderIp, Subject)

  If _raw_log contains syslog-wrapped positional data:
    Use regextract() to strip syslog prefix, then split() + arrayindex()

  If data is in label/value array pairs:
    Use regextract() with pattern targeting the label/value structure

3.3 MAP SOURCE FIELDS TO XDM

Prioritise in this order:
  1. xdm.observer.vendor (hardcoded string) and xdm.observer.product (hardcoded string)
  2. xdm.event.type -- normalised category: "ALERT", "NETWORK", "AUTH", "EMAIL", "FILE", "PROCESS", "ENDPOINT_ACTIVITY", "AUDIT"
  3. xdm.event.id, xdm.event.original_event_type, xdm.event.description, xdm.event.outcome
  4. Source and target fields: IPs, hostnames, ports, users
  5. Domain-specific fields: alert.*, email.*, network.*, auth.*
  6. Intermediate fields: for proxy/gateway devices between source and target

3.4 WRITE THE RULE

Structure with clear stages:
  Stage 1: Extract intermediary (_temp) fields from the raw log
  Stage 2 (optional): Build composite fields (descriptions, decomposed values)
  Stage 3: Assign XDM fields using the intermediaries

3.5 VALIDATE (MANDATORY CHECKLIST)

  Every _temp variable is consumed in an XDM assignment (unused = blocking error)
  xdm.observer.vendor and xdm.observer.product are set (hardcoded strings)
  xdm.event.type is set to a normalised category string
  XDM_CONST values are NOT quoted
  Dataset name is NOT quoted in the MODEL header
  No leading pipe before the first stage after MODEL header
  Numeric comparisons use numeric literals (severity = 4 not severity = "4")
  Array-type XDM fields use arraycreate()
  to_string() wraps any arrayindex() output before passing to split() or regextract()
  No self-referencing XDM fields (xdm.x = coalesce(xdm.x, _y) is INVALID)
  No chained arrow operators (column -> field -> subfield is INVALID; use json_extract_scalar)
  Rule ends with semicolon
  No trailing comma before the semicolon
  Null guard filter is present
  Every XDM field path exists in the schema above


========================================================================
SECTION 4: CRITICAL PITFALLS (MISTAKES THAT CAUSE VALIDATION FAILURES)
========================================================================

PITFALL: Unused temp field
  WRONG: _unused = something (never assigned to XDM)
  FIX: Remove it or map it to an XDM field

PITFALL: String vs number comparison
  WRONG: severityNumber = "4"
  FIX: severityNumber = 4

PITFALL: Quoted XDM_CONST
  WRONG: "XDM_CONST.OUTCOME_SUCCESS"
  FIX: XDM_CONST.OUTCOME_SUCCESS (no quotes)

PITFALL: Quoted dataset name
  WRONG: dataset="name_raw"
  FIX: dataset=name_raw

PITFALL: Self-referencing XDM field in assignment
  WRONG: xdm.target.ipv4 = coalesce(xdm.target.ipv4, _fallback)
  FIX: xdm.target.ipv4 = _fallback
  REASON: XDM fields have no value when the assignment executes in a MODEL rule

PITFALL: Chained arrow operator
  WRONG: imperva -> ids -> site_name
  FIX: json_extract_scalar(to_string(imperva), "$.ids.site_name")
  REASON: Chained arrow syntax is invalid for nested JSON access

PITFALL: Missing to_string() wrap
  WRONG: split(arrayindex(_parts, 3), "/")
  FIX: split(to_string(arrayindex(_parts, 3)), "/")
  REASON: arrayindex returns a variant type; split and regextract require string input

PITFALL: Array field without arraycreate
  WRONG: xdm.email.recipients = _recipient
  FIX: xdm.email.recipients = if(_recipient != null, arraycreate(_recipient), null)
  REASON: Array-type fields require array values

PITFALL: Leading pipe on first stage
  WRONG: [MODEL: dataset=x_raw]\n| alter
  FIX: [MODEL: dataset=x_raw]\nalter  (or filter)

PITFALL: Missing terminal semicolon
  WRONG: xdm.observer.vendor = "Foo"
  FIX: xdm.observer.vendor = "Foo";

PITFALL: from_epoch (does not exist)
  WRONG: from_epoch(_ts, "MILLIS")
  FIX: parse_epoch(_ts, "MILLIS")

PITFALL: Non-existent XDM field
  WRONG: xdm.source.process.parent_process.name
  FIX: This field does not exist in the schema. Do not use it.

PITFALL: Trailing comma before semicolon
  WRONG: xdm.observer.vendor = "Foo",;
  FIX: xdm.observer.vendor = "Foo";

PITFALL: Unused non-underscore intermediary fields (BLOCKING ERROR)
  Every non-underscore-prefixed field extracted in an alter block MUST be referenced in a subsequent xdm.* assignment (directly or transitively). The Cortex IDE treats this as a blocking validation error: "Data Model Rules contains unused fields". This is NOT a warning -- it is a hard block on both _raw and _gc_raw datasets.

PITFALL: Unguarded parse_epoch / parse_timestamp
  WRONG: _time = parse_epoch(_timestamp_ms, "MILLIS")
  FIX: _time = if(_timestamp_ms != null and _timestamp_ms != "", parse_epoch(_timestamp_ms, "MILLIS"), null)
  REASON: If the input is null or empty, parse_epoch throws a runtime error

PITFALL: _time assignment in MODEL rules
  WRONG: _time = parse_epoch(...) in a MODEL rule
  FIX: Remove it. _time must only be set during INGEST (parsing) rules.

PITFALL: Invalid JSON path with @ prefix
  WRONG: json_extract_scalar(_raw_log, "$.@timestamp")
  FIX: json_extract_scalar(_raw_log, "$['@timestamp']")
  REASON: Keys with special characters like @ must use bracket notation


========================================================================
SECTION 5: EXTRACTION PATTERNS
========================================================================

PATTERN 1: JSON Extraction from Parsed Columns

When _raw_log is empty and the ingest parser produces top-level JSON string columns, extract nested fields using json_extract_scalar with to_string wrapping:

  _field = json_extract_scalar(to_string(column_name), "$.nested.path")


PATTERN 2: Direct Column Access

When _raw_log is empty and fields are available as direct top-level columns with no nesting, use column names directly:

  xdm.email.subject = Subject,
  xdm.source.ipv4 = senderIp


PATTERN 3: Syslog + Positional Extraction

When _raw_log contains syslog-wrapped space-delimited data:

  Step 1: Strip syslog prefix using regextract
    _stripped = arrayindex(regextract(_raw_log, "Info:\s+(.+)$"), 0)

  Step 2: Split by space
    _parts = split(_stripped, " ")

  Step 3: Access positional fields
    _field0 = arrayindex(_parts, 0)
    _field1 = arrayindex(_parts, 1)

  Step 4: Decompose composite tokens (MUST wrap in to_string first)
    _sub_a = arrayindex(split(to_string(_field0), "/"), 0)
    _sub_b = arrayindex(split(to_string(_field0), "/"), 1)


PATTERN 4: Label/Value Array Extraction

When data is in [{"label": "X", "value": Y}, ...] format inside a JSON column:

  For string values:
    _field = arrayindex(regextract(msg, "\"Label Name\"\s*,\s*\"value\"\s*:\s*\"([^\"]+)\""), 0)

  For numeric values:
    _field = arrayindex(regextract(msg, "\"Label Name\"\s*,\s*\"value\"\s*:\s*(\d+)"), 0)


PATTERN 5: Coalesce for Multi-Path Fields

When the same logical data appears under different field names depending on event type:

  _sender_ip = coalesce(senderIp, SourceIP)
  _recipient = coalesce(recipients, Recipient)


PATTERN 6: Single-Entity Mirroring

When a payload has only one IP or one user, map to BOTH source and target for maximum correlation coverage in XSIAM:

  xdm.source.ipv4 = _client_ip,
  xdm.target.ipv4 = _client_ip,
  xdm.source.user.username = _user,
  xdm.target.user.username = _user

Only do this when there is genuinely a single entity. When source and target are different entities (e.g. email sender vs recipient, web client vs upstream server), do NOT mirror.


PATTERN 7: Severity Normalisation

Normalise vendor severity labels to proper case for xdm.alert.severity:

  xdm.alert.severity = if(
      _risk_level = "low", "Low",
      _risk_level = "medium", "Medium",
      _risk_level = "high", "High",
      _risk_level = "critical", "Critical",
      _risk_level != null, _risk_level)


PATTERN 8: Structured Event Description

Build a human-readable summary using concat() with conditional sections:

  xdm.event.description = concat(
      "Vendor ", eventType,
      if(direction != null, concat(" (", direction, ")"), ""),
      if(Subject != null, concat(" | Subject: ", Subject), ""),
      if(Action != null, concat(" | Action: ", Action), ""))


PATTERN 9: Positional/Syslog Log Extraction (Full Example)

When _raw_log contains a syslog-wrapped positional log:

  Step 1: Strip syslog wrapper
    _stripped = arrayindex(regextract(_raw_log, "Info:\s+(.+)$"), 0)

  Step 2: Split and extract positional fields
    _parts = split(_stripped, " ")
    _client_ip = arrayindex(_parts, 2)
    _result_status = arrayindex(_parts, 3)
    _http_method = arrayindex(_parts, 5)

  Step 3: Decompose composite fields
    _cache_result = arrayindex(split(to_string(_result_status), "/"), 0)
    _http_status = arrayindex(split(to_string(_result_status), "/"), 1)

  Key rules:
    Always wrap arrayindex() output in to_string() before split() or regextract()
    Hyphen "-" means empty in Squid format. Check field != "-" before assigning to XDM
    Syslog hostname gives the observer/intermediary device identity


PATTERN 10: Defensive coalesce(PascalCase, camelCase)

When the XSIAM parser may produce field names in either PascalCase or camelCase (common with AWS, Azure, GCP sources), use coalesce on both forms throughout:

  finding_resource = coalesce(Resource, resource)
  finding_id = coalesce(Id, id)
  resource_instance_id = coalesce(finding_resource -> InstanceDetails.InstanceId, finding_resource -> instanceDetails.instanceId)


PATTERN 11: XDM_CONST Mapping Chains

When a source field value must be mapped to an XDM constant enumeration, use a full if() chain covering all possible values:

  xdm.source.user.identity_type = if(
      resource_user_type = "Root", XDM_CONST.IDENTITY_TYPE_BUILTIN,
      resource_user_type = "IAMUser", XDM_CONST.IDENTITY_TYPE_USER,
      resource_user_type = "AssumedRole", XDM_CONST.IDENTITY_TYPE_MACHINE,
      resource_user_type = "FederatedUser", XDM_CONST.IDENTITY_TYPE_VIRTUAL,
      resource_user_type != null, XDM_CONST.IDENTITY_TYPE_UNKNOWN)


PATTERN 12: Directional IP/Port Resolution

When a finding reports both local and remote IPs with a direction indicator (INBOUND/OUTBOUND), resolve source and target based on direction:

  source_ipv4 = if(is_inbound, remote_ip, is_outbound, local_ip, fallback_ip)
  target_ipv4 = if(is_inbound, local_ip, is_outbound, remote_ip)


PATTERN 13: Transitive Field Usage

Intermediary fields may feed into other intermediary fields before reaching an XDM assignment. This is valid as long as the chain terminates in an xdm.* assignment:

  http_code = to_integer(raw_status_code)
  xdm.network.http.response_code = if(http_code = 200, XDM_CONST.HTTP_RSP_CODE_OK, ...)

If http_code were extracted but NOT mapped, Cortex rejects the rule.


========================================================================
SECTION 6: KNOWN XDM COMPATIBILITY ISSUES
========================================================================

IMPORTANT: Fields listed here are NOT valid for general use. Do NOT treat this
section as a list of available XDM fields. These are fields that cause errors.
Only use fields from Section 2.

6.1 xdm.alert.mitre_techniques (validator WARN-023; promoted 2026-04)

  Symptom:    The Cortex IDE validator emits WARN-023 ("XDM_CONST enum
              chain too large") and on _gc_raw datasets the validator's
              type-checker crashes with an "internal error" cascade.
              On non-_gc_raw datasets the warning is non-blocking but
              still appears on every rule that maps the field.
  Datasets:   Always fires on _gc_raw datasets (BLOCKING).
              Fires as a non-blocking warning on every other dataset
              (extrahop_revealx_raw, imperva_*_raw, panw_iot_raw,
              novasec_*_raw, etc.).
  Workaround: 1. On _gc_raw datasets, OMIT xdm.alert.mitre_techniques
                 entirely. Map xdm.alert.mitre_tactics only -- the
                 tactic enum is small enough for the validator.
              2. On non-_gc_raw datasets, the field is still required
                 by the spec because correlation depends on technique
                 mapping. Accept the WARN-023 as the floor signal.
              3. NEVER work around this by inventing your own technique
                 string field -- the warning is on the canonical path
                 and there is no alternative XDM sink for technique
                 enumeration.
  Reference:  IDE rule WARN-023, validated 2026-04 against the live
              tenant. The verified ExtraHop reference rule emits this
              warning intrinsically.

6.2 xdm.target.process.integrity_level

  Crashes Cortex IDE validator on _gc_raw datasets (XDM_CONST enum
  mapping crash). Same root cause as 6.1 (oversized enum). Omit on
  _gc_raw; safe to map on other datasets if needed.

6.3 xdm.session_context_id

  Incompatible on _gc_raw datasets (causes internal error). Omit on
  _gc_raw; the field exists in the schema but is not part of the
  _gc_raw data model layer.

6.4 xdm.network.direction

  Not part of the selected data model on _gc_raw datasets. Omit.

6.5 xdm.source.process.parent_process.*

  Fields do NOT exist in the XDM schema at all. Always fails on every
  dataset. Use xdm.source.process.* for the immediate process and do
  not attempt to model the parent chain in this XDM category.

Root cause analysis: The Cortex IDE "internal error" message is a generic fallback for at least three distinct problems:
  1. Non-existent XDM fields (always fail on every dataset)
  2. Dataset-incompatible fields (exist in schema but not in the dataset's data model)
  3. XDM_CONST enum mapping crashes (valid field + valid constant but validator cannot handle the chain)

Debugging methodology: When Cortex IDE returns "internal error", use binary search to isolate the offending field. Start minimal (3-5 fields), add in batches, halve the failing batch until you find the single problematic field.


