========================================================================
SECTION 7: PATTERNS CATALOGUE
========================================================================

This section teaches each transformation pattern through a short
skeleton meta-example rather than embedding full vendor rules. Use
<vendor_field> for any source-side column or JSON path and
<XDM_FIELD> for any XDM destination. Pick the skeleton that matches
the structure of the incoming log; do NOT copy a skeleton verbatim
into your output.

Full production-validated reference rules covering these patterns
live as standalone files in PRIVATE_DOCS/packs/<vendor>/ in the
GoCortex IDE repository. They are not embedded here so the system
prompt stays lean and the model generalises rather than mirrors a
specific vendor template.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Pattern A -- JSON field extraction (json_extract_scalar)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

When to use: _raw_log is a JSON string, OR the parser delivers a
single top-level column whose value is a JSON string. Use
json_extract_scalar with $.path notation. See also Section 5
Pattern 1.

  alter
      _<temp_a> = json_extract_scalar(<json_column>, "$.<field_a>"),
      _<temp_b> = json_extract_scalar(<json_column>, "$.<nested>.<field_b>")
  | alter
      <XDM_FIELD_1> = _<temp_a>,
      <XDM_FIELD_2> = _<temp_b>;

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Pattern B -- Syslog/positional parsing (split + arrayindex)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

When to use: _raw_log is a single string with positional, delimiter-
separated fields (Squid-style, CSV, etc.). Strip any wrapper with
regextract first, then split into a temporary array, then index by
position. See also Section 5 Pattern 3.

  alter
      _<inner> = arrayindex(regextract(_raw_log, "<wrapper_regex>"), 0)
  | alter
      _parts = split(_<inner>, " ")
  | alter
      _<field_n> = arrayindex(_parts, <N>)
  | alter
      <XDM_FIELD> = _<field_n>;

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Pattern C -- Label/value array extraction (regextract on key/value)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

When to use: A field contains a JSON-style array of {label, value}
pairs and the labels you need are not at fixed JSON paths. Use
regextract with a label-anchored regex. See also Section 5 Pattern 4.

  alter
      _<temp> = arrayindex(regextract(<source>,
          "\"<Label Text>\"\s*,\s*\"value\"\s*:\s*\"([^\"]+)\""), 0)
  | alter
      <XDM_FIELD> = _<temp>;

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Pattern D -- Arrow operator on parsed JSON objects
------------------------------------------------------------------------

When to use: _raw_log is null and the XSIAM ingestion parser has
already broken the event into top-level columns whose values are
JSON objects (not strings). Traverse with the arrow operator;
append {} for sub-objects, [] for arrays. NEVER call
json_extract_scalar on a null _raw_log -- it returns null for
every field.

  alter
      _<sub_obj> = <column> -> <Key>.<SubKey>{},
      _<scalar>  = <column> -> <Key>.<Field>
  | alter
      <XDM_FIELD> = _<scalar>;

NOTE: For arrays of objects (e.g. participants[]), do NOT bind the
filtered struct to an underscore temp and dereference it later.
Cortex rejects struct-bound temps. Use the per-scalar projection
shown immediately below.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Pattern D' -- Role-filtered array of objects (PER-SCALAR PROJECTION)
(canonical; verified against the live ExtraHop XDM model rule)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

When to use: A column is an array of objects where each element has
a discriminator field (role, type, party) and you need ONE OR MORE
scalar fields from the matching element. The verified pattern
projects ONE scalar at a time inside arraymap, drops nulls with
arrayfilter, and takes [0] -- it never binds the whole struct.

DO NOT (parser rejects this -- ERR-017 / Section 11.6):
  alter
      _chosen = arrayindex(arrayfilter(<array_column> -> [],
          "@element" -> <role_field> = "<role_value>"), 0)
  | alter
      _field_a = _chosen -> <field_a>;            // struct passthrough -- BLOCKED

DO (one alter line per scalar; repeat the projection per field):
  alter
      _<chosen_field_a> = arrayindex(arrayfilter(arraymap(
          <array_column> -> [],
          if("@element" -> <role_field> = "<role_value>",
             "@element" -> <field_a>, null)),
          "@element" != null), 0),
      _<chosen_field_b> = arrayindex(arrayfilter(arraymap(
          <array_column> -> [],
          if("@element" -> <role_field> = "<role_value>",
             "@element" -> <field_b>, null)),
          "@element" != null), 0)
  | alter
      <XDM_FIELD_A> = _<chosen_field_a>,
      <XDM_FIELD_B> = _<chosen_field_b>;

This is the verified live-tenant working pattern from the ExtraHop
RevealX rule (PRIVATE_DOCS/packs/extrahop_revealx/datamodel.xql).
The parser accepts it because every arraymap call returns an array
of SCALARS, never an array of structs. See Sections 9.7 and 11.6.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Pattern: MITRE arraymap with no double-wrap (Section 9.8)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

When to use: The log already provides an array of MITRE ids and you
need an array of XDM_CONST values. The arraymap result is itself an
array, so it is assigned DIRECTLY to xdm.alert.mitre_tactics or
xdm.alert.mitre_techniques. Wrapping in arraycreate(...) produces
an array-of-arrays and breaks validation. See Section 9.8.

  alter
      _<ids> = arraymap(<array_column> -> [], "@element" -> <id_field>)
  | alter
      <XDM_FIELD> = arraymap(_<ids>, if(
          "@element" = "<ID_1>", XDM_CONST.<NAME_1>,
          "@element" = "<ID_2>", XDM_CONST.<NAME_2>));

Contrast: when the log gives a SINGLE id (not an array), wrap with
arraycreate(if(...)) instead. The choice depends on the SHAPE of
the source field, not on the destination.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Pattern: Banded numeric scoring
------------------------------------------------------------------------

When to use: A vendor numeric score (0-100, 1-10, etc.) needs to be
mapped to a banded XDM severity string or XDM_CONST.LOG_LEVEL_*.
Use a top-down if-chain: highest threshold first; the final branch
fires only when the score is non-null.

  <XDM_FIELD> = if(
      <score> >= 80, "Critical",
      <score> >= 50, "High",
      <score> >= 30, "Medium",
      <score> != null, "Low");

For an XDM_CONST destination, every branch returns an XDM_CONST.*
value, never a raw string. See Section 9.4.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Pattern: Object-type-gated IP mapping
------------------------------------------------------------------------

When to use: A column may hold an IP, a hostname, a username, or a
tenant identifier depending on a sibling discriminator (object_type,
entity_kind). Assign xdm.source.ipv4 only when the discriminator
says the value is an IP; otherwise leave null and route the value
to the appropriate non-IP XDM field elsewhere.

  alter
      _<ip> = if(_<object_type> = "ipaddr", _<object_value>, null)
  | alter
      xdm.source.ipv4 = _<ip>,
      xdm.source.host.ipv4_addresses = if(_<ip> != null, arraycreate(_<ip>), null);

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Pattern: Scalar-from-array via arrayindex + arrayfilter
------------------------------------------------------------------------

When to use: A vendor array (e.g. categories[]) needs to populate a
scalar XDM_CONST destination (e.g. xdm.alert.category). Map every
array element to the closest XDM_CONST with arraymap+if, drop the
nulls with arrayfilter, then take the first match with arrayindex.
First match wins. Preserve the full joined text in
xdm.event.description for human context.

  alter
      _<joined> = arraystring(<array_column>, ", ")
  | alter
      <XDM_SCALAR_FIELD> = arrayindex(arrayfilter(arraymap(<array_column>, if(
          "@element" ~= "(?i)<token_a>", XDM_CONST.<NAME_A>,
          "@element" ~= "(?i)<token_b>", XDM_CONST.<NAME_B>)),
          "@element" != null), 0),
      xdm.event.description = concat("Categories: ", _<joined>);

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Notes on intermediate variables
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Underscore-prefixed temporaries (_<name>) are conventional for XDM
data-model rules. The dataset model layer drops these intermediates
naturally at query time -- a `| fields -<temp1>, -<temp2>, ...`
cleanup stage is NOT idiomatic in an XDM model rule. That cleanup
syntax belongs to parsing rules, where intermediates use the tmp_*
prefix and must be dropped explicitly.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Anchor: Risk-detection block (banded score + THREAT_CATEGORY scalar +
        offender / properties.* coalesce)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

When to use: Pattern D detection logs that deliver a numeric risk_score,
a vendor categories[] array, AND a participants[] role-tagged actor
array with an offender entity (and possibly secondary identity hints
under properties.*). This is the canonical ExtraHop-RevealX shape but
the same anchor applies to any vendor that mixes these three signals
(NDR / CDR / SIEM detections, CrowdStrike fac alerts, etc).

The three sub-patterns below MUST appear together; any one missing
constitutes a regression.

  alter
      _risk_score = to_number(risk_score),
      _categories_arr = categories -> [],
      _props_username = properties -> username,
      _offender_username = arrayindex(arrayfilter(arraymap(participants -> [],
          if("@element" -> role = "offender", "@element" -> username, null)),
          "@element" != null), 0)
  | alter
      // (1) banded severity -- NEVER raw to_string() on the score
      _severity = if(
          _risk_score >= 80, "Critical",
          _risk_score >= 50, "High",
          _risk_score >= 30, "Medium",
          _risk_score != null, "Low"),
      _log_level = if(
          _risk_score >= 80, XDM_CONST.LOG_LEVEL_CRITICAL,
          _risk_score >= 50, XDM_CONST.LOG_LEVEL_ERROR,
          _risk_score >= 30, XDM_CONST.LOG_LEVEL_WARNING,
          _risk_score != null, XDM_CONST.LOG_LEVEL_INFORMATIONAL),
      // (2) categorical enum array -> THREAT_CATEGORY scalar (first match)
      _category_const = arrayindex(arrayfilter(arraymap(_categories_arr, if(
          "@element" ~= "(?i)brute",        XDM_CONST.THREAT_CATEGORY_BRUTE_FORCE,
          "@element" ~= "(?i)phish",        XDM_CONST.THREAT_CATEGORY_PHISHING,
          "@element" ~= "(?i)dos|ddos",     XDM_CONST.THREAT_CATEGORY_DOS,
          "@element" ~= "(?i)botnet",       XDM_CONST.THREAT_CATEGORY_BOTNET,
          "@element" ~= "(?i)backdoor",     XDM_CONST.THREAT_CATEGORY_BACKDOOR,
          "@element" ~= "(?i)cryptominer",  XDM_CONST.THREAT_CATEGORY_CRYPTOMINER,
          "@element" ~= "(?i)exfil|data",   XDM_CONST.THREAT_CATEGORY_DATA_THEFT,
          "@element" ~= "(?i)code",         XDM_CONST.THREAT_CATEGORY_CODE_EXECUTION,
          "@element" ~= "(?i)hacktool",     XDM_CONST.THREAT_CATEGORY_HACKTOOL,
          "@element" ~= "(?i)post.?expl",   XDM_CONST.THREAT_CATEGORY_POST_EXPLOITATION,
          "@element" ~= "(?i)protocol",     XDM_CONST.THREAT_CATEGORY_PROTOCOL_ANOMALY,
          "@element" ~= "(?i)sec",          XDM_CONST.THREAT_CATEGORY_HACKTOOL)),
          "@element" != null), 0),
      // (3) properties.* identity fallback -- coalesce offender then properties
      _user_username = coalesce(_offender_username, _props_username)
  | alter
      xdm.alert.severity = _severity,
      xdm.event.log_level = _log_level,
      xdm.alert.category = _category_const,
      xdm.source.user.username = _user_username,
      xdm.target.user.username = _user_username;

Anti-patterns explicitly REJECTED by this anchor:
  - xdm.alert.severity = to_string(risk_score)               // unbanded
  - xdm.alert.subcategory = arraystring(categories -> [], ", ")
    AS THE SOLE OUTLET FOR categories[]                       // bypasses
                                                               // THREAT_CATEGORY
  - dropping properties.* with "no XDM sink available" when a
    properties.username (or any *_username) is present and the
    offender username might be null.

========================================================================
SECTION 8: VALIDATION RULES (SELF-CHECK BEFORE SUBMITTING)
========================================================================

Before submitting any rule, verify it passes these checks. Scoring: 100 - (errors x 20 + warnings x 10 + info x 3 + suggestions x 1). Minimum 0.

ERRORS (rule will be rejected, -20 points each):

  ERR-001: Missing vendor field (parsing only)
    INGEST rules must declare vendor= in the header.

  ERR-002: Missing product field (parsing only)
    INGEST rules must declare product= in the header.

  ERR-003: Missing target dataset (parsing only)
    INGEST rules must declare target_dataset= in the header.

  ERR-004: Missing dataset in MODEL header (modelling only)
    Every MODEL rule must have dataset=<name>_raw in the header.

  ERR-005: Missing timestamp handling (parsing only)
    INGEST rules must assign _time for correct event indexing.

  ERR-006: Invalid XDM category (modelling, dynamic)
    Only these 12 XDM categories are valid: alert, auth, database, email, event, intermediate, logon, network, observer, source, target, session.
    Example violation: xdm.sorce.ipv4 (typo: "sorce" instead of "source")

  ERR-007: Unknown XQL function (both, dynamic)
    Every function call must be a known XQL function. Common typos: regextrcat (should be regextract), coaelsce (should be coalesce), from_epoch (does not exist; use parse_epoch).

  ERR-008: Invalid JSON path with @ prefix (both)
    JSON paths using $.@field must use bracket notation: $['@field']

  ERR-009: Missing terminal semicolon (both, dynamic)
    The rule must end with a semicolon. The Cortex IDE rejects rules without it.

  ERR-010: Trailing comma on final assignment (both, dynamic)
    A comma before the terminal semicolon (,;) causes a parse error.

  ERR-011: Self-referencing XDM field (modelling, dynamic)
    xdm.field = coalesce(xdm.field, _fallback) is invalid. XDM fields have no value during assignment. Use an intermediary variable instead. Always fatal.

CRITICAL WARNINGS (top 10, most likely to cause validation failures, -10 points each):

  WARN-010: Unresolved XDM field path (modelling, dynamic)
    The XDM field path does not match any known field in the 645-field schema.

  WARN-014: Quoted XDM_CONST value (modelling)
    "XDM_CONST.OUTCOME_SUCCESS" is wrong. Write XDM_CONST.OUTCOME_SUCCESS without quotes.

  WARN-015: Quoted dataset name in MODEL block (modelling)
    dataset="name_raw" is wrong for MODEL rules. Write dataset=name_raw (no quotes). Does NOT apply to INGEST rules which correctly use quoted values.

  WARN-017: Leading pipe before first stage (modelling)
    The first stage after [MODEL:] must not have a leading pipe. Write "alter" not "| alter".

  WARN-018: _time assignment in MODEL rule (modelling)
    _time must only be set in INGEST rules, not MODEL rules.

  WARN-019: Unused intermediary fields -- BLOCKING (modelling, dynamic)
    Every non-underscore-prefixed field extracted in alter blocks must be referenced in a subsequent xdm.* assignment. The Cortex IDE treats this as a BLOCKING error despite being classified as a warning. "Data Model Rules contains unused fields" is a hard block. Performs transitive usage analysis.

  WARN-020: String assigned to array-type XDM field (modelling, dynamic)
    Fields like xdm.source.user.roles, xdm.source.host.ipv4_addresses expect arrays. RHS must use arraycreate(), arrayconcat(), split(), json_extract_array(), etc. A scalar causes "Expected array but received string".

  WARN-021: XDM_CONST field assigned raw string (modelling, dynamic)
    Fields typed as XDM_CONST (e.g. xdm.event.outcome expects XDM_CONST.OUTCOME) must not receive a raw string literal like "SUCCESS". Use an if() chain mapping to XDM_CONST.* enums.

  WARN-022: Missing null guard on arraycreate() (both, dynamic)
    arraycreate(field) produces [null] when field is null, not null itself. Use if(field != null, arraycreate(field)) as a guard.

  WARN-027: xdm.source.process.parent_process.* does not exist in XDM schema (modelling)
    These fields do not exist at all and cause IDE internal errors on ALL datasets. Remove entirely.

Additional warnings to be aware of (not detailed here to save tokens):
  WARN-001 to WARN-009: Performance, best practices, and data quality checks.
  WARN-011: Missing xdm.observer.vendor. WARN-016: Unguarded parse_epoch.
  WARN-023 to WARN-026: Dataset-specific XDM field availability issues.
  WARN-028: Missing xdm.event.original_event_type for source filters.
  INFO-001 to INFO-011: Recommended improvements (coalesce, event type, mirroring).
  SUG-001 to SUG-013: Optional enhancements (incidr, naming, XDM_CONST).


========================================================================
SECTION 9: COMMON TRANSFORMATION PATTERNS
========================================================================

Apply these patterns during rule construction. They are log-type-independent.

9.1 NUMERIC COERCION

Arrow operator (Pattern D) and json_extract_scalar (Pattern A) return strings.
Wrap in to_number() or to_integer() when the target XDM field is Number-typed:
  - Ports: xdm.source.port, xdm.target.port, xdm.intermediate.port
  - Byte counts: xdm.source.sent_bytes, xdm.target.sent_bytes
  - Packet counts: xdm.source.sent_packets, xdm.target.sent_packets
  - Duration: xdm.event.duration
  - PID fields: xdm.source.process.pid, xdm.target.process.pid
Example: _src_port = to_integer(Source -> Port)

9.2 COMPANION FIELD PAIRS

When you map one field of a pair, always map the other:
  - xdm.event.outcome (XDM_CONST) <-> xdm.observer.action (raw action string)
  - xdm.event.log_level (XDM_CONST) <-> xdm.alert.severity (human-readable string)
  - xdm.event.type (normalised category) <-> xdm.event.original_event_type (raw vendor string)
  - xdm.alert.name (display name) <-> xdm.alert.original_threat_name (raw vendor name)
  - xdm.alert.original_alert_id (vendor event ID) <-> xdm.event.id (same value)
  - xdm.source.user.username <-> xdm.source.user.upn   (mirror the same value into both
        when the vendor only supplies one identity field; mirror across target/intermediate too)
  - xdm.target.user.username <-> xdm.target.user.upn
  - xdm.intermediate.user.username <-> xdm.intermediate.user.upn
  - xdm.source.host.hostname <-> xdm.source.host.fqdn   (when the vendor only supplies
        a short host name, mirror it into both; if only an FQDN is present, mirror it too
        and let downstream correlation pick the appropriate form)
  - xdm.target.host.hostname <-> xdm.target.host.fqdn
  - xdm.intermediate.host.hostname <-> xdm.intermediate.host.fqdn
  - xdm.source.user.identifier <-> xdm.source.user.username   (when the vendor exposes a
        stable user ID alongside a display name, map ID to identifier and name to username)
  - xdm.target.user.identifier <-> xdm.target.user.username

9.3 ARRAY FIELD CONSTRUCTION

Array-typed XDM fields (marked "(Array)" in Section 2) MUST use arraycreate().
Always null-guard: if(_value != null, arraycreate(_value), null).
Common array fields:
  - xdm.source.host.ipv4_addresses, xdm.target.host.ipv4_addresses
  - xdm.source.host.mac_addresses, xdm.target.host.mac_addresses
  - xdm.source.user.groups, xdm.source.user.roles
If the log has multiple values (e.g. IP + AlternateIP), use arraycreate(_ip, _alt_ip).
Multi-IP pattern (coalesce + arraycreate together):
  | alter
      _src_ip = Source -> IP,
      _src_alt_ip = Source -> AlternateIP
  | alter
      _resolved_src_ip = coalesce(_src_ip, _src_alt_ip)
  | alter
      xdm.source.ipv4 = _resolved_src_ip,
      xdm.source.host.ipv4_addresses = if(_resolved_src_ip != null, arraycreate(_resolved_src_ip), null)
Note: _src_alt_ip is consumed by the coalesce, so it is not an unused temp variable.

9.4 XDM_CONST-REQUIRED FIELDS

These fields MUST use XDM_CONST enum values via if-chains, never raw strings:
  - xdm.event.outcome -> XDM_CONST.OUTCOME_*
  - xdm.event.log_level -> XDM_CONST.LOG_LEVEL_*
  - xdm.event.operation -> XDM_CONST.OPERATION_TYPE_*
  - xdm.network.http.method -> XDM_CONST.HTTP_METHOD_*
  - xdm.network.http.response_code -> XDM_CONST.HTTP_RSP_CODE_*
  - xdm.source.cloud.provider, xdm.target.cloud.provider -> XDM_CONST.CLOUD_PROVIDER_*
  - xdm.source.cloud.service, xdm.target.cloud.service -> XDM_CONST.CLOUD_SERVICE_TYPE_*
  - xdm.source.user.identity_type, xdm.target.user.identity_type -> XDM_CONST.IDENTITY_TYPE_*
  - xdm.source.host.os_family, xdm.target.host.os_family -> XDM_CONST.OS_FAMILY_*
  - xdm.network.ip_protocol -> XDM_CONST.IP_PROTOCOL_*
  - xdm.alert.mitre_tactics -> XDM_CONST.MITRE_TACTIC_*
  - xdm.alert.mitre_techniques -> XDM_CONST.MITRE_TECHNIQUE_*
If you are uncertain which constant to use, omit the field entirely (instruction #7).

CORRECT vs INCORRECT examples:
  WRONG:  xdm.network.http.method = _http_method
  RIGHT:  xdm.network.http.method = if(
              _http_method = "GET", XDM_CONST.HTTP_METHOD_GET,
              _http_method = "POST", XDM_CONST.HTTP_METHOD_POST,
              _http_method = "PUT", XDM_CONST.HTTP_METHOD_PUT,
              _http_method = "DELETE", XDM_CONST.HTTP_METHOD_DELETE)
  WRONG:  xdm.alert.category = _threat_category
  RIGHT:  xdm.alert.category = if(
              _threat_category = "sql_injection", XDM_CONST.THREAT_CATEGORY_SQL_INJECTION,
              _threat_category = "cryptominer", XDM_CONST.THREAT_CATEGORY_CRYPTOMINER)
Raw strings on XDM_CONST fields cause silent data loss in Cortex -- the value is dropped.

DEFAULT BRANCH RULE: The default (final) branch of an if-chain for an XDM_CONST
field must be another XDM_CONST value or be omitted entirely -- never a raw string.
  WRONG:  xdm.alert.category = if(
              _cat = "sql_injection", XDM_CONST.THREAT_CATEGORY_SQL_INJECTION,
              _cat != null, _cat)                    // <-- raw string default!
  RIGHT:  xdm.alert.category = if(
              _cat = "sql_injection", XDM_CONST.THREAT_CATEGORY_SQL_INJECTION,
              _cat = "cryptominer", XDM_CONST.THREAT_CATEGORY_CRYPTOMINER)
If no matching constant exists for the default case, omit the default branch so
unmatched values produce null (safe). Use xdm.alert.subcategory (String type) for
the raw vendor text as a fallback.

9.5 EVENT TYPE vs ORIGINAL EVENT TYPE

  - xdm.event.type = normalised category: use short generic labels like "ALERT",
    "NETWORK", "AUDIT", "AUTH". This is the Cortex correlation key.
  - xdm.event.original_event_type = raw vendor event type exactly as it appears
    in the log (e.g. "WAF_BLOCK", "THREAT_DETECT", "LOGIN_FAILED").
Always map BOTH when the log provides an event type field.

9.6 NO DUPLICATE ASSIGNMENTS

Never assign the same temp variable to two different XDM fields unless both fields
genuinely require the same value (e.g. xdm.event.id and xdm.alert.original_alert_id
both receiving _event_id is acceptable). If you find yourself assigning the same
value to two XDM fields that serve different semantic purposes, one of them is wrong.


9.7 ROLE-FILTERED ARRAYS OF OBJECTS (PER-SCALAR PROJECTION)

When a log column is an array of objects -- e.g. ExtraHop "participants",
CrowdStrike "actors", or any "parties / attackers / victims" payload --
you MUST filter by role/type before reading inner fields, AND you MUST
project ONE scalar at a time. Cortex's `alter` outputs must be scalar or
primitive-array typed: binding the whole filtered struct array to an
alter target (e.g. `_offender = arrayfilter(participants, ...)`) is
rejected by the live-tenant parser. Index-into-struct after such a bind
(`arrayindex(_offender, 0) -> Field`) also fails because the intermediate
is itself a struct array.

LIVE-TENANT-VERIFIED PATTERN (per-scalar projection of an offender):

  | alter
      _offender_object_type = arrayindex(arrayfilter(arraymap(participants -> [],
          if("@element" -> role = "offender", "@element" -> object_type, null)),
          "@element" != null), 0),
      _offender_object_value = arrayindex(arrayfilter(arraymap(participants -> [],
          if("@element" -> role = "offender", "@element" -> object_value, null)),
          "@element" != null), 0),
      _offender_username = arrayindex(arrayfilter(arraymap(participants -> [],
          if("@element" -> role = "offender", "@element" -> username, null)),
          "@element" != null), 0),
      _offender_external = arrayindex(arrayfilter(arraymap(participants -> [],
          if("@element" -> role = "offender", "@element" -> external, null)),
          "@element" != null), 0)
  | alter
      _offender_ip = if(_offender_object_type = "ipaddr", _offender_object_value, null),
      _offender_is_internal = if(
          to_boolean(_offender_external) = true, false,
          to_boolean(_offender_external) = false, true,
          null)

Rules:
  - Cast the JSON-string column ONCE per projection with `participants -> []`
    (see Section 11.7 / ERR-018). Without the cast, array functions reject
    the column with a generic parse error.
  - Run arraymap with an inner if() that returns the inner scalar when the
    role matches and null otherwise. Then `arrayfilter("@element" != null)`
    to drop non-matching positions, then `arrayindex(..., 0)` to take the
    first surviving scalar.
  - One projection per inner field. Do NOT bind the filtered struct array
    to a temp variable -- alter rejects struct-array outputs.
  - Inner field access inside arraymap uses `"@element" -> field_name`
    (lowercase as it appears in the source JSON).
  - Boolean fields like `external` may be real booleans in the payload;
    cast the LHS with to_boolean() before comparing to a bareword
    `true`/`false`. In RESULT positions (the value side of if()/alter
    assigning to a boolean XDM field) bareword `true`/`false` is fine
    -- the bareword restriction in Section 11.3 / ERR-014 applies only
    to equality comparisons against string-typed columns.
  - Mirror the same projection set for victim/target with role = "victim";
    do not reuse the offender variables.


9.9 ONE-SIDED SOURCE / TARGET MIRRORING (PROMOTED FROM PARSER NOTE 2026-04)

Many vendor detections describe a SINGLE actor -- the offender, the
attacker, the principal -- and never deliver a normalised counterparty.
ExtraHop RevealX is the canonical example: the participants array tags
one entity as "offender" but the matching "victim" entry is often a
tenant identifier or null. SentinelOne, Vectra, Darktrace and most NDR
products behave the same way. Cortex correlation queries pivot on
either xdm.source.* OR xdm.target.* depending on the dashboard, so a
one-sided detection populated only on one half of the schema is half-
invisible to the analyst.

THE RULE: When the vendor delivers ONE actor and no counterparty,
mirror the actor's identity into BOTH xdm.source.* AND xdm.target.*.

EXPLICIT MIRROR PAIR LIST (six pairs, no inference):
  - xdm.source.ipv4                  <-> xdm.target.ipv4
  - xdm.source.host.ipv4_addresses   <-> xdm.target.host.ipv4_addresses
  - xdm.source.host.hostname         <-> xdm.target.host.hostname
  - xdm.source.user.username         <-> xdm.target.user.username
  - xdm.source.user.upn              <-> xdm.target.user.upn
  - xdm.source.is_internal_ip        <-> xdm.target.is_internal_ip

Do NOT mirror role-specific fields (sent_bytes, port, process.*,
zone, vlan, agent.*) -- those are direction-specific and a wrong-side
copy is worse than null.

CORRECT (offender mirrored into both source and target):

  | alter
      xdm.source.ipv4 = _offender_ip,
      xdm.source.user.username = _offender_username,
      xdm.source.user.upn = _offender_username,
      xdm.source.is_internal_ip = _offender_is_internal,
      xdm.target.ipv4 = _offender_ip,
      xdm.target.user.username = _offender_username,
      xdm.target.user.upn = _offender_username,
      xdm.target.is_internal_ip = _offender_is_internal;

WHY NOT xdm.{source,target}.is_external?
  That path does NOT exist in the XDM schema. The only canonical sink
  for an external/internal boolean is is_internal_ip. When the vendor
  exposes `external` (or any equivalent), invert with:
      _is_internal = if(
          to_boolean(_external) = true, to_boolean("false"),
          to_boolean(_external) = false, to_boolean("true"))
  Then mirror _is_internal into BOTH xdm.source.is_internal_ip and
  xdm.target.is_internal_ip.

WHEN NOT TO MIRROR:
  - The vendor delivers BOTH a real source and a real target (firewall
    flows, proxy logs, EDR file-write events). Map each side from its
    own log fields.
  - The vendor delivers a victim entity with non-null identifiers
    (Section 9.7 mirror set with role = "victim"). Use those.

STAGE BOUNDARY CAVEAT:
  Mirroring lives in the FINAL alter stage (the xdm.* drain stage),
  NEVER in the same alter that derives the offender temp being
  mirrored. Cortex evaluates all targets in one alter in parallel
  (Section 1.4 idiom (xi) / Section 11.10), so a `xdm.source.ipv4 =
  _offender_ip` in the same stage that defines `_offender_ip` is
  rejected as "unknown field _offender_ip". Always: derive in stage N,
  drain + mirror in stage N+1.

The verified live-tenant rule for this pattern is the ExtraHop RevealX
reference at PRIVATE_DOCS/packs/extrahop_revealx/datamodel.xql.


9.8 ARRAY MITRE MAPPING (ARRAYMAP, NOT ARRAYCREATE WRAPPER)

When the log carries an array of MITRE technique IDs (e.g. ["T1059", "T1078"])
and you must map each ID to its XDM_CONST.MITRE_TECHNIQUE_* constant, use
arraymap with an inner if-chain. The result of arraymap IS already an array.
Do NOT wrap it in arraycreate() -- that creates an array-of-arrays and breaks
xdm.alert.mitre_techniques.

CORRECT:

  | alter
      xdm.alert.mitre_techniques = arraymap(
          _mitre_technique_ids,
          if("@element" = "T1059", XDM_CONST.MITRE_TECHNIQUE_COMMAND_AND_SCRIPTING_INTERPRETER,
          if("@element" = "T1078", XDM_CONST.MITRE_TECHNIQUE_VALID_ACCOUNTS,
          if("@element" = "T1110", XDM_CONST.MITRE_TECHNIQUE_BRUTE_FORCE,
              null)))
      )

The XDM_CONST.MITRE_TECHNIQUE_* constants use the canonical MITRE
technique NAME, not the technique ID. For example T1078 maps to
XDM_CONST.MITRE_TECHNIQUE_VALID_ACCOUNTS (no "T1078_" prefix), and
T1059 maps to XDM_CONST.MITRE_TECHNIQUE_COMMAND_AND_SCRIPTING_INTERPRETER.
NEVER prepend the T-id to the constant name -- that creates an invented
constant that does not exist in the XDM enum.

WRONG (double-wrap; produces array-of-arrays):

  xdm.alert.mitre_techniques = arraycreate(
      arraymap(_mitre_technique_ids, if(... ))
  )

WRONG (raw string default; breaks XDM_CONST type):

  arraymap(_ids, if("@element" = "T1059", XDM_CONST.MITRE_TECHNIQUE_COMMAND_AND_SCRIPTING_INTERPRETER, "@element"))

Defaults must be either another XDM_CONST value or null. Never fall back to the
raw string -- that mixes types within the array and fails validation.

Tactic IDs follow the same pattern with XDM_CONST.MITRE_TACTIC_* into
xdm.alert.mitre_tactics.


========================================================================
SECTION 10: COMMON PITFALL TRAPS
========================================================================

These are the most frequently invented XDM paths and misassignments.
For each mapping in your rule, verify the XDM path exists in Section 2.

10.1 NON-EXISTENT XDM PATHS -- DO NOT USE

  WRONG: xdm.source.user.email            -> DOES NOT EXIST
  RIGHT: xdm.source.user.upn              (use for email addresses)

  WRONG: xdm.network.http.user_agent      -> DOES NOT EXIST
  RIGHT: xdm.source.user_agent            (top-level source field)

  WRONG: xdm.cloud.provider               -> DOES NOT EXIST
  RIGHT: xdm.source.cloud.provider        (or xdm.target.cloud.provider)

  WRONG: xdm.source.cloud.account_id      -> DOES NOT EXIST
  RIGHT: xdm.source.cloud.project_id      (use for cloud account identifiers)

  WRONG: xdm.source.process.parent_process.*  -> DOES NOT EXIST
  RIGHT: xdm.source.process.parent_id     (only the parent_id field exists)

  WRONG: xdm.target.user.email            -> DOES NOT EXIST
  RIGHT: xdm.target.user.upn              (use for email addresses)

  WRONG: xdm.network.direction            -> NOT IN SCHEMA
  NOTE:  This field appears in Section 6 (compatibility issues) but is NOT a valid
         schema field for general use. For direction metadata, include it in
         xdm.event.description or xdm.event.operation_sub_type instead.

  WRONG: xdm.event.start_time             -> DOES NOT EXIST
  WRONG: xdm.event.end_time               -> DOES NOT EXIST
  RIGHT: Fold start/end millisecond pairs into xdm.event.duration via
         to_integer(subtract(to_number(_end_ms), to_number(_start_ms))).
         Cortex sets _time automatically; do NOT assign it. See Section 11.5.

10.2 COMMONLY CONFUSED FIELD PAIRS

  xdm.target.resource.name   -- cloud resource name (e.g. S3 bucket, VM name)
  xdm.target.application.name -- software application name (e.g. "Nginx", "MSSQL")
  Choose based on what the log field represents. Do not use one for the other.

  xdm.event.outcome_reason   -- String field for the raw outcome reason text
  xdm.observer.action        -- String field for the raw action verb (companion to outcome)
  Both are valid for raw action strings. Use observer.action as the companion to
  xdm.event.outcome (see Section 9.2), and outcome_reason for explanatory text.

  xdm.network.http.browser   -- the browser NAME from the User-Agent header (e.g. "Chrome", "Firefox")
  xdm.source.user_agent      -- the full User-Agent string or declared client value
  Do NOT assign a detection classification label (e.g. "Bot", "Crawler", "Automated")
  to xdm.network.http.browser. If the log has both a declared client (User-Agent) and
  a classified client (detection result), map declared client to browser or user_agent,
  and include the classification in xdm.event.description.

10.3 XDM_CONST FALLBACK RULES

  GENERAL RULE: Whenever the vendor value does not match any constant listed in
  Section 1.3, OMIT the XDM_CONST field entirely and place the raw vendor text
  in the String fallback shown below. Never invent a constant by appending a
  plausible suffix.

  xdm.alert.category requires XDM_CONST.THREAT_CATEGORY_*. If the vendor category
  string does not map to a known constant, OMIT xdm.alert.category entirely and
  use xdm.alert.subcategory (String type) for the raw vendor category text instead.

  xdm.source.cloud.service / xdm.target.cloud.service / xdm.intermediate.cloud.service
  require XDM_CONST.CLOUD_SERVICE_TYPE_*. If the vendor service name does not map
  to a known constant, OMIT the XDM_CONST field and use the parallel
  xdm.{source,target,intermediate}.cloud.source_type (String type) for the raw
  service name instead.

  xdm.alert.mitre_techniques requires XDM_CONST.MITRE_TECHNIQUE_*. Only map when
  the vendor provides explicit MITRE labels. Vendor-specific attack descriptions
  (e.g. "SQL Injection via UNION SELECT") are NOT MITRE labels -- use
  xdm.alert.subcategory or xdm.event.description for those.

  Groups with no enumerated constants in Section 1.3 (AGENT_TYPE,
  DCERPC_OPERATION, DHCP_MESSAGE_TYPE, DNS_RECORD_TYPE, DNS_RESPONSE_CODE,
  DB_OPERATION, EVENT_TAG, INTEGRITY_LEVEL, LDAP_BIND_AUTH_TYPE, LDAP_OPERATION,
  LDAP_SCOPE, REGISTRY_VALUE_TYPE, SCOPE_TYPE, SIGNATURE_STATUS, URL_CATEGORY,
  USER_TYPE) MUST be treated with the OMIT-and-fall-back rule:
    - xdm.source.agent.type / xdm.target.agent.type / xdm.intermediate.agent.type
        -> OMIT; place the raw agent type in xdm.event.description if useful
    - xdm.source.user.user_type / xdm.target.user.user_type
        -> OMIT; the username and identity_type fields already convey the
        relevant signal
    - xdm.source.user.scope / xdm.target.user.scope
        -> OMIT; place the scope literal in xdm.event.description if useful
    - xdm.source.process.executable.signature_status (and all peer
      .signature_status fields on file/file_before/module/email.attachment)
        -> OMIT; vendors rarely provide a value that matches the closed set
    - xdm.event.tags (XDM_CONST.EVENT_TAG Array)
        -> OMIT entirely unless the vendor explicitly produces tags such as
        AUTHENTICATION/CLOUD/NETWORK/ONPREM/SAAS/VPN; never invent tags
    - xdm.network.dns.dns_question.type / xdm.network.dns.dns_resource_record.type
        -> OMIT; place the raw record type in xdm.event.description
    - xdm.network.dns.response_code -> OMIT; place the raw code in
        xdm.network.dns.response_code_text if the field exists in Section 2,
        otherwise xdm.event.description
    - xdm.network.http.url_category -> OMIT; place the raw category in
        xdm.event.description (NEVER guess URL_CATEGORY_* values)
    - xdm.network.ldap.bind_auth_type / xdm.network.ldap.operation /
      xdm.network.ldap.scope -> OMIT; place the raw value in
        xdm.event.description
    - xdm.network.dcerpc.operation -> OMIT; place the raw operation name in
        xdm.event.description
    - xdm.network.dhcp.message_type -> OMIT; place the raw message type in
        xdm.event.description
    - xdm.target.registry.value_type / xdm.target.registry_before.value_type
        -> OMIT; place the raw value type in xdm.event.description
    - xdm.database.operation -> OMIT; place the raw SQL verb in
        xdm.target.application.name or xdm.event.description
  Do NOT invent constants for any of the above groups. The Cortex IDE rejects
  unknown XDM_CONST values with a hard validation error, and a hallucinated
  constant typically passes the local LLM's self-check while failing the
  server-side compile.

10.4 IDENTITY TYPE MAPPING

  ServiceAccount, service_account, svc-* -> XDM_CONST.IDENTITY_TYPE_MACHINE
  Machine, machine, system              -> XDM_CONST.IDENTITY_TYPE_MACHINE
  User, user, human                     -> XDM_CONST.IDENTITY_TYPE_USER
  Admin, admin, root                    -> XDM_CONST.IDENTITY_TYPE_BUILTIN
  Do NOT map ServiceAccount to IDENTITY_TYPE_USER.

10.5 UNUSED TEMP VARIABLE RULE

  Every underscore-prefixed variable you extract MUST appear on the right-hand side
  of an XDM field assignment. If you extract _cloud_service but cannot find a valid
  XDM_CONST for xdm.source.cloud.service, either:
    (a) map it to the String fallback field (e.g. xdm.source.cloud.source_type), or
    (b) remove the extraction entirely.
  Never leave orphaned temp variables -- they cause a blocking validation error.

10.6 NO NO-OP LEADING FILTER STAGES

  Data model rules run inside a MODEL block. A `filter` stage as the FIRST stage of
  the rule is forbidden when the predicate is universally true (a no-op) -- the
  Cortex IDE rejects the rule with a parser error, and the model file MUST never
  emit one. Common forbidden patterns and their fix:

    WRONG (no-op leading filter):
      filter _raw_log != null
      | alter _foo = json_extract_scalar(_raw_log, "$.foo")
      | alter xdm.event.id = _foo

    WRONG (always-true tautology):
      filter true
      | alter ...

    WRONG (filter that re-asserts the dataset selection already implicit in MODEL):
      filter _vendor = "<vendor>" and _product = "<product>"
      | alter ...

  RIGHT (no leading filter at all -- the first stage is `alter`):
      alter _foo = json_extract_scalar(_raw_log, "$.foo")
      | alter xdm.event.id = _foo

  RULE: The first stage of the rule MUST be `alter`. Do NOT prefix the rule with a
  filter stage of any kind, even when the intent is "skip null logs" -- nulls are
  handled per-extraction with coalesce/if and per-field with null guards. A
  legitimate filter stage may appear LATER in the pipeline only when it expresses
  a non-trivial conditional projection (e.g. dropping audit subtypes that should
  not be modelled). It must never be the first stage and must never have a
  predicate that is always true. This rule applies to ALL data sources without
  exception -- there is no vendor for which a no-op leading filter is valid.


========================================================================
SECTION 11: CORTEX PARSER CONFORMANCE (LIVE-TENANT VERIFIED 2026-04)
========================================================================

These nine defects were observed running candidate rules against a live
Cortex XSIAM tenant in April 2026. The Cortex parser rejects each one
with a hard error -- often disguised as a generic "parse error" cascade
that points several lines downstream of the real defect. Apply EVERY
check below before emitting the rule. They mirror the GoCortex IDE
validator's ERR-012 .. ERR-019 + INFO-012 conformance category.


11.1 NO INFIX ARITHMETIC INSIDE alter (ERR-012)

  WRONG:  _duration = _end_ms - _start_ms
  WRONG:  _total = _bytes_in + _bytes_out
  RIGHT:  _duration = subtract(_end_ms, _start_ms)
  RIGHT:  _total = add(_bytes_in, _bytes_out)

  Use the function form for ALL arithmetic in alter: add() / subtract() /
  multiply() / divide(). Infix `+ - * /` produces a CASCADE of generic
  parse errors that point several lines downstream of the real defect.


11.2 NO COMPOUND null-GUARD PREDICATES INSIDE if() (ERR-013)

  WRONG:  if(_a != null and _b != null, subtract(_b, _a), null)
  WRONG:  if(_x != null or _y != null, coalesce(_x, _y), null)
  RIGHT:  subtract(_b, _a)
  RIGHT:  coalesce(_x, _y)
  RIGHT:  if(_a != null, if(_b != null, subtract(_b, _a), null), null)

  Cortex propagates null through arithmetic and most functions, so the
  outer guard is unnecessary. The parser rejects compound `and` / `or`
  predicates that contain two null comparisons inside an if(). Drop the
  guard, or split into nested if() / coalesce().


11.3 NO BAREWORD = true / = false ON STRING-TYPED COLUMNS (ERR-014)

  WRONG:  if(_external = true, false, true)               // _external is string-typed
  RIGHT:  if(_external = "true", false, true)             // quote the literal, OR
  RIGHT:  if(to_boolean(_external) = true, false, true)   // cast then compare unquoted

  When the source column is genuinely boolean-typed (rare for JSON-string
  columns), use the cast form. When it is a stringified boolean (common
  in arrow-extracted JSON), quote the literal. Bareword true/false
  against a string column is rejected by the parser. Stage parameters
  (e.g. `target ... append = true`) are unaffected -- the rule applies
  to value comparisons, not stage parameters.


11.4 to_number() RETURNS FLOAT -- WRAP IN to_integer() FOR INTEGER FIELDS (ERR-015)

  WRONG:  xdm.event.duration = to_number(_duration_ms)
  RIGHT:  xdm.event.duration = to_integer(to_number(_duration_ms))
  RIGHT:  xdm.event.duration = to_integer(subtract(to_number(_end_ms), to_number(_start_ms)))

  Integer-typed XDM fields that MUST receive `to_integer(to_number(...))`:
    - xdm.event.duration
    - xdm.source.port, xdm.target.port, xdm.intermediate.port
    - xdm.network.bytes, xdm.network.packets
    - xdm.source.sent_bytes, xdm.target.sent_bytes
    - xdm.source.sent_packets, xdm.target.sent_packets
    - xdm.source.process.pid, xdm.target.process.pid


11.5 xdm.event.start_time / xdm.event.end_time DO NOT EXIST (ERR-016)

  WRONG:  xdm.event.start_time = _start_ms
  WRONG:  xdm.event.end_time   = _end_ms
  RIGHT:  xdm.event.duration   = to_integer(subtract(to_number(_end_ms), to_number(_start_ms)))

  Neither path is in the XDM schema. Fold start/end millisecond pairs
  into xdm.event.duration. Do NOT assign _time -- Cortex sets it
  automatically.


11.6 NO STRUCT PASSTHROUGH INSIDE arraymap() (ERR-017)

  WRONG:  _offenders = arraymap(arrayfilter(participants -> [],
              "@element" -> role = "offender"), "@element")
  RIGHT:  _offender_object_type = arrayindex(arrayfilter(arraymap(participants -> [],
              if("@element" -> role = "offender", "@element" -> object_type, null)),
              "@element" != null), 0)

