  via `"@element" -> field_name`. Returning the whole struct ("@element"
  on its own) is rejected by the parser, and binding a struct array to
  an alter target is also rejected. See Section 9.7 for the full
  per-scalar projection pattern.


11.7 CAST JSON-STRING COLUMNS WITH `-> []` BEFORE ARRAY FUNCTIONS (ERR-018)

  WRONG:  arraymap(participants, ...)
  WRONG:  arrayfilter(categories, ...)
  WRONG:  arraystring(mitre_tactics, ", ")
  RIGHT:  arraymap(participants -> [], ...)
  RIGHT:  arrayfilter(categories -> [], ...)
  RIGHT:  arraystring(mitre_tactics -> [], ", ")

  Columns that arrive as JSON-string literals (commonly `participants`,
  `categories`, `mitre_tactics`, `mitre_techniques`, `properties`,
  `tags`, `filters`, `detail`) MUST be cast with the `-> []` array
  projection before being passed to any array function (arraymap,
  arrayfilter, arraystring, arraydistinct, array_length). For nested
  object access, use `column -> field_name` instead.


11.8 EVERY UNDERSCORE TEMP MUST REACH AN xdm.* ASSIGNMENT (ERR-019)

  Every `_var` you define must, through any chain of intermediary
  assignments, eventually appear on the RHS of an `xdm.*` assignment.
  Cortex rejects orphans on `_gc_raw` datasets with the message
  "Data Model Rules contains unused fields" -- this is a HARD BLOCK,
  not a warning. The chain may pass through both underscore-prefixed
  and bare intermediary names, and through multi-line if() / concat() /
  arraymap() bodies. Before writing the rule, scan every `_var =` and
  trace it to an xdm.* consumer. If you cannot, delete the extraction.


11.9 CASCADE ROOT CAUSE -- FIX THE EARLIEST DEFECT FIRST (INFO-012)

  When the Cortex parser reports two or more errors on adjacent lines,
  the EARLIEST one is almost always the root cause. The remaining
  errors are downstream cascade noise from the parser losing its
  position. Fix the first defect, recompile, and the rest typically
  vanish.

  Most common cascade roots (in priority order):
    1. Section 11.1 -- infix arithmetic in alter
    2. Section 11.6 -- arraymap struct passthrough
    3. Section 11.7 -- missing `-> []` cast on a JSON-string column
    4. Section 11.2 -- compound null-guard predicate inside if()
    5. Section 11.3 -- bareword true/false on a string column

  Do NOT chase the line number Cortex reports -- walk back to the
  nearest preceding `<lhs> =` line and start there.
